<h2 id="what">What this site is, and is not</h2>
<p>
  The site republishes the harmful algal bloom advisory records that state
  agencies publish, and, for some large lakes, satellite estimates from the
  US Environmental Protection Agency, labeled as estimates. The
  <a href="{{ root }}about/#sources">about page</a> names every source. It is
  not a government site, and it is not affiliated with or endorsed by any
  state or federal agency.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>This site is not a safety determination.</strong> It never tells you
  whether water is fit for swimming, wading, fishing, drinking or letting a
  dog in. No advisory on a page does not mean a water is free of toxic algae:
  most waters are never tested. Always follow posted signs at the water and
  the instructions of the agency that manages it, and check the official map
  before you go. If you think a person or an animal has been exposed, contact
  a doctor or a veterinarian. Nothing here is medical or veterinary advice.
</p>

<h2 id="data">The data can be wrong or late</h2>
<p>
  The records come from the agencies, and they can be incomplete, wrong or
  out of date. Conditions at a water can change within hours, long before
  any record does. This site reads the agencies' data on a schedule, so it
  can be behind them, and the copying can add errors of its own.
</p>

